---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="The Prophet | Library | Amitoj Singh" description="Kahlil Gibran's timeless wisdom on love, work, freedom, and the integration of opposites.">
  <article class="container-zen section-zen">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <a href="/library" class="text-ink-muted hover:text-ink mb-8 inline-block">&larr; Back to Library</a>

      <h1 class="mb-2 text-balance">The Prophet</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-ink-muted mb-6">Kahlil Gibran</p>

      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 text-sm text-ink-muted mb-8">
        <span>3-5 hours</span>
        <span class="text-oat-400">&middot;</span>
        <span>Accessible</span>
        <span class="text-oat-400">&middot;</span>
        <span>Philosophy, Spirituality, Wisdom</span>
      </div>

      <div class="accent-line mb-8"></div>

      <!-- WHY I RECOMMEND THIS -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-sm text-ink-muted uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">Why I Recommend This</h2>
        <div class="prose prose-lg">
          <p>Someone giving this book at twenty. Understanding it at forty. The images didn't change; the capacity to receive them did. Pillars standing apart to hold a temple made no sense when independence seemed like a failure of commitment. Children as arrows shot from the bow felt cold when I wanted control to equal love. Joy and sorrow filling the same well just sounded poetic.</p>
          <p>Now those images land differently. Not because Gibran's words shifted, but because the questions they address became real rather than theoretical. How to hold intimacy without collapsing into fusion. How to let work carry meaning without demanding outcomes. How to integrate opposites instead of choosing sides. Poetic form turns out to do something analysis can't—it holds complexity without resolving it into principles. That matters when teaching about consciousness, when the precision required isn't conceptual but experiential.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- THE BOOK -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-sm text-ink-muted uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">The Book</h2>
        <div class="prose prose-lg">
          <p>Almustafa, a prophet who has lived twelve years in exile, prepares to return home. The people of Orphalese gather and ask him to speak on the essential questions of human existence: love, marriage, children, work, freedom, pain, death. Twenty-six teachings delivered in prose poetry, framed as farewell wisdom.</p>
          <p>The genius lies in what historian Juan Cole called "dogma-free universal spiritualism." Gibran integrates apparent opposites into both/and consciousness. Love both crowns and crucifies. Joy and sorrow rise from the same well. Reason and passion serve as the soul's rudder and sails—both needed, neither sufficient alone. Written in 1923, the book has sold over 100 million copies and been translated into more than 100 languages. It anticipated the Third Enlightenment's core principles a century early.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- PASSAGES -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-sm text-ink-muted uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">Passages That Stayed With Me</h2>
        <div class="space-y-6">
          <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-sage-400 pl-6">
            <p class="text-lg italic mb-2">"Your children are not your children. They are the sons and daughters of Life's longing for itself. They come through you but not from you, And though they are with you yet they belong not to you."</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">On releasing control and honoring emergence.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-sage-400 pl-6">
            <p class="text-lg italic mb-2">"Work is love made visible."</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">The antidote to the meaning crisis.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-sage-400 pl-6">
            <p class="text-lg italic mb-2">"Your pain is the breaking of the shell that encloses your understanding. Even as the stone of the fruit must break, that its heart may stand in the sun, so must you know pain."</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">Growth requires breaking through old forms.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-sage-400 pl-6">
            <p class="text-lg italic mb-2">"Let there be spaces in your togetherness, And let the winds of the heavens dance between you."</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">Independence within intimacy, like pillars supporting a temple.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-sage-400 pl-6">
            <p class="text-lg italic mb-2">"No man can reveal to you aught but that which already lies half asleep in the dawning of your knowledge."</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">Teaching as awakening, not imposing. The threshold of your own mind.</p>
          </blockquote>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- READ THIS IF -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-sm text-ink-muted uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">Read This If...</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-ink-light">
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-sage-600">&bull;</span>
            <span>You seek wisdom that transcends religious boundaries while honoring spiritual depth</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-sage-600">&bull;</span>
            <span>You wrestle with integrating opposites—reason and passion, freedom and commitment, joy and sorrow</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-sage-600">&bull;</span>
            <span>You write or teach about consciousness and want to see how poetic images convey what analysis cannot</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-sage-600">&bull;</span>
            <span>You need language for the meaning crisis—for work as purpose, for relationships that honor both connection and autonomy</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- SKIP THIS IF -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-sm text-ink-muted uppercase tracking-wider mb-4">Skip This If...</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-ink-light">
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-oat-400">&bull;</span>
            <span>You prefer systematic philosophy to poetic wisdom</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex gap-3">
            <span class="text-oat-400">&bull;</span>
            <span>You find aphorisms too open to interpretation, wanting concrete prescriptions instead</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <div class="pt-8 border-t border-oat-300">
        <p class="text-ink-muted text-sm mb-3">Related</p>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-2 mb-4">
          <a href="/third-enlightenment" class="text-sm text-sage-700 hover:text-sage-900">The Third Enlightenment — on integrating opposites and finding meaning beyond optimization &rarr;</a>
        </div>
        <a href="/library" class="text-sage-700 hover:text-sage-900 font-medium">&larr; Back to Library</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .prose p {
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }
</style>
